comments.body:
> That would make each call site one line

This may be true in Rust, but I don't think this is true in C++. Recall that the clang-format doesn't allow stuff like `if (auto r = args.GetArg("-foo", 0, 100); !r) return util::Error{...}; else if (*r) opts.foo = **r;`

Also, the alternative, where the result is assigned in the outer scope (before the if) is ugly and tedious, because the result will leak the scope after the if handling.

Finally, passing the min and max without named args will be confusing, when normally the second arg meant the default value.  Also, having to redundantly pass the min/max when not needed seems redundant.


So the better option would be:

```cpp
template <std::integral Int>
struct GetArgOptions {
    Int min{std::numeric_limits<Int>::min()};
    Int max{std::numeric_limits<Int>::max()};
};

template <std::integral Int>
std::optional<util::Result<Int>> GetArg(const std::string& name, GetArgOptions<Int> opts = {}) const;

```

This would result in code like:

```cpp
    if (const auto foo = args.GetArg<u16>("-foo", {.max = 100})) {
        if (!*foo) return util::Error{util::ErrorString(*foo)};
        opts.foo = **foo;
// foo is not leaked outside this scope, nice
    }
